    {
        "id": "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h4",
        "type": "hint",
        "dependencies": [
            "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h3"
        ],
        "title": "Recall the law of exponents",
        "text": "Recall the law of exponents for products with different bases and the same exponent: $$(a**b)(c**b)=(ac)**b.$$",
        "variabilization": {},
        "oer": "",
        "license": ""
    },
    {
        "id": "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h5",
        "type": "hint",
        "dependencies": [
            "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h4"
        ],
        "title": "Apply the law of exponents",
        "text": "$$\\log_2\\left(x**{1/2}(x-3)**{1/2}\\right)=\\log_2\\left(x(x-3)\\right)**{1/2}$$",
        "variabilization": {},
        "oer": "",
        "license": ""
    },
    {
        "id": "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h6",
        "type": "hint",
        "dependencies": [
            "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h5"
        ],
        "title": "Exponentiate both sides with base $$2$$. If $$a=b$$, $$then$$ $$c**a=c**b.$$",
        "text": "Exponentiate both sides of $$\\log_2\\left(x(x-3)\\right)**{1/2}=1,$$ which gives $$2**{\\log_2\\left(x(x-3)\\right)**{1/2}}=2**1.$$",
        "variabilization": {},
        "oer": "",
        "license": ""
    },
    {
        "id": "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h7",
        "type": "hint",
        "dependencies": [
            "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h6"
        ],
        "title": "Apply cancelation property",
        "text": "Exponentiation cancels logarithms: $$b**{\\log_b(a)}=a.$$ $$2**{\\log_2\\left(x(x-3)\\right)**{1/2}}=\\left(x(x-3)\\right)**{1/2}=2**1$$",
        "variabilization": {},
        "oer": "",
        "license": ""
    },
    {
        "id": "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h8",
        "type": "hint",
        "dependencies": [
            "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h7"
        ],
        "title": "Sqare both sides and solve for $$x$$",
        "text": "$$\\left(x(x-3)\\right)**{{1/2}**2}=2**2$$ \\n $$x(x-3)=4$$ \\n $$x^2-3x-4=0$$ \\n $$\\left(x-4\\right) \\left(x+1\\right)=0$$ \\n This implies that $$x=4$$, $$x=-1$$ are solutions.",
        "variabilization": {},
        "oer": "",
        "license": ""
    },
    {
        "id": "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h9",
        "type": "hint",
        "dependencies": [
            "a19b4c7A251-simplifylogexpressions-P04a-h8"
        ],
        "title": "Find solutions in domain of original equation",
        "text": "Now we must ask, are both solutions possible inputs for the original equation? Recall the original formulation: \\n $$\\frac{1}{2}\\log_2(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_2(x-3)=1.$$ The function $$\\log_2(x)$$ cannot take negative-valued input. So the only answer that makes sense for this equation is $$x=4$$.",
        "variabilization": {},
